I smell a use-mention confusion here, though it is hard to pin down.  xorxe=
s is=20
quite careful in his remarks about 'lerfu', but some other points are less=
=20
clear. As usual, the problem seems to circle around the core.  A bridi is a=
=20
predicate, therefore  a word or phrase.  A selbri is then the abstract enti=
ty, a=20
relation, to which the bridi refers (or, maybe. means).  A sumti is then a=
=20
object which is involved in the selbri relation, a relatum.  A brivla is a =
word=20
that can serve as a bridi.  The definition of 'tanru' is then suspect since=
 it=20
groups words (brivla) to form not a phrase (bridi) but a relation (selbri).=
=20
 Similarly, a gadri is said to transform a selbri, a relation, into a sumti=
, a=20
relatum.  Further, a cmevla is said to be  a word usable as a name ; but ev=
ery=20
word is usable as a name, to this not quite what is wanted.  And cmene is s=
aid=20
to be a way of referring, rather than something that refers in a certain wa=
y.=20
 Sorting all this out seems a tedious task but maybe necessary, since I thi=
nk=20
all of the usages mentioned here actually occur from time to time in the=20
literature.
